-- title:
--   Cost bound for the radix sort of tagged rows
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a list of rows of width $w \ge 1$ (integers of bit length $\le B$) stored flattened on stack 0, with stack 54 holding $|R|$ and stack 3 holding $kb$, where $kb+2\le B$ and $\mathrm{zsz}(|R|w), \mathrm{zsz}(w)\le B$. Then the sorting program `sortW w` (a $kb$-pass binary radix sort on the first entry of each row) costs at most $2\cdot10^5(kb+1)(|R|w+1)(B+2) + 9000(B+2)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.sortW_cost` of the Lean development NLogN/HTrans.lean (lines 1102–1137), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

